      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "During the lumber boom of the 1880s, Manistee was the headquarters of Manistee and North‑Eastern railroad and had more millionaires per capita than anywhere else in the U.S.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manistee%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Ramsdell Theatre, built in 1938, is an Art Deco venue that once hosted the Manistee Civic Players and later drew filmmaker Michael Moore for a restoration push.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manistee%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Manistee’s name comes from an Ojibwe word meaning “river with islands at its mouth,” reflecting the city’s location on the Manistee River’s delta.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manistee%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Three major factories—Packaging Corporation of America, Morton Salt, and Martin Marietta—anchor Manistee’s industrial landscape on Lake Manistee.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manistee%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Manistee National Forest surrounds the city, offering thousands of acres of forest, lakes, and rivers that define the region’s natural beauty.",
